          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-9e24d1a23b93eff0\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Berto directly avows that he loves Loredana, using that state to explain and excuse his unintended shift to the intimate “tu.” The sentence asserts his love but does not itself undertake a commitment. Although he says he could not control himself, this refers principally to the preceding familiar address; the love declaration is a deliberate explanatory addition, not clearly a reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"“Non ho saputo dominarmi” creates slight pressure toward E, but the narration says Berto notices her reaction and then adds the explanation, favoring a controlled avowal rather than an involuntary verbal outburst.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Berto directly tells Loredana “Vi amo” during their conversation, as part of an apology for addressing her with the intimate singular pronoun.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Berto has already declared tender love and offered to devote his life to Loredana, while also gazing at and touching her possessively. Here he apologizes after perceiving that his intimate “tu” has offended her and presents his love as the cause of his lost self-control. This courtship and self-exculpatory context may affect how his motives are judged, but the core utterance remains a straightforward avowal rather than a new undertaking. Loredana rejects his approach and later explicitly says that she hates him, underscoring that the avowal is neither reciprocated nor accepted.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Vi chiedo scusa; non volevo offendervi. Vi amo, e non ho saputo dominarmi.”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present “Vi amo” directly presents Berto's loving state as true, strongly supporting T; its placement within an apology makes it explanatory rather than promissory.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration says that Loredana experiences Berto's preceding “tu” as brutal; Berto notices this and then adds the apology and love avowal.\",\"supports\":\"Berto formulates the declaration responsively after recognizing her offense, which weighs against treating the words themselves as a reflex-like discharge under E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Berto says, “posso dedicarvi intera la mia vita” and “Vi amo teneramente,” while Loredana answers, “Non vi amerò mai.”\",\"supports\":\"The wider exchange establishes an ongoing, unreciprocated courtship. Commitments appear elsewhere, but the highlighted declaration itself does not undertake one.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately afterward Loredana rises and says, “mi lasci andare!”\",\"supports\":\"Her rejection shows that the avowal functions within an unwanted advance, without changing its core truth-presenting mode.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the direct avowal. The apologetic, self-exculpatory, passionate, and unreciprocated context can be described separately without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The score concerns utterance force, not the sincerity of Berto's professed love or the ethical character of his conduct.\"}"
        }
